// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// Dust DDS roundtrip bench app: the row `dust` of the speed setup
// (tests/perf/speed/speed.py, rc8-plan.md, arc 3, decision 1, D3). The same
// modes, environment and output lines as the C++ apps of this directory
// (bench_common.hpp) and the HDDS app (../hdds), over the dust_dds API, the
// Rust API Dust DDS 0.16.0 is written in.
//
// Type: roundtrip.idl, generated by build.rs with dust_dds_gen (the IDL code
// generator of Dust DDS). dust_dds_gen maps `sequence<octet, 65536>` to
// `Vec<u8>` and drops the bound (`sequence_type` in dds_gen/src/generator/
// rust.rs), so the type Dust announces has an unbounded payload; the
// serialized sample is the same (a uint32 length and the bytes).
//
// Samples are taken in a listener, as in the other apps. Dust's own code
// does both: a listener in dds/examples/best_effort_subscriber.rs and in
// best_effort_write_and_receive of dds/benches/benchmark.rs, a WaitSet in
// dds/examples/ping.rs and pong.rs.
//
// What differs from the C++ apps, because the Dust API has no counterpart:
// - the DataReaderListener is async: Dust calls it on its executor thread
//   (one per process, dds/src/std_runtime/executor.rs, the thread that also
//   runs the participant) with a DataReaderAsync, and the listener takes
//   with `take(..).await`. A call of the sync API there would wait on that
//   same thread, so the pong writes its echo with DataWriterAsync::write.
//   The writers are created through the async API for that reason (with the
//   block_on below); the main thread writes through the sync DataWriter that
//   wraps the same writer (DataWriter::from, which is what
//   Publisher::create_datawriter returns);
// - Dust calls on_data_available once per sample it adds to the reader's
//   cache (dds/src/dcps/dcps_domain_participant/communication_methods.rs);
//   each call takes what the cache holds, and a call whose sample an earlier
//   take has taken finds none;
// - DataWriter::write takes the sample by value: the app clones its sample
//   for each write. The ping stamps t_send_ns after the clone, as Dust's own
//   ping example (dds/examples/ping.rs) starts its clock after `data.clone()`;
// - XCDR2 offered and accepted, as the C++ apps do by default (Dust's own
//   default is an empty DataRepresentation, XCDR1);
// - Dust DDS 0.16.0 has one transport, RTPS over UDPv4 (feature
//   `rtps_udp_transport`): ZERODDS_BENCH_TRANSPORT set to anything but UDPv4
//   (SHM among them) is refused with exit code 2.
//
// Under KEEP_ALL the DataWriter of Dust DDS 0.16.0 keeps every sample it
// has written: it removes samples from its history under KEEP_LAST only
// (dds/src/dcps/dcps_domain_participant/writer_methods.rs), so with
// RESOURCE_LIMITS max_samples N every write after the N-th returns
// OutOfResources, acknowledged or not (dds/src/dcps/dcps_domain_participant/
// data_writer_entity.rs). With KEEP_ALL, max_samples 1000 and 1 KiB this app
// wrote 1000 samples and every later write failed, under BEST_EFFORT and
// under RELIABLE with acked=1. The app counts such writes as `failed`.

